const QA={
py:[
[`List vs tuple?`,`Lists are mutable; tuples are immutable. Tuples are hashable (if their contents are), so they can be dict keys, and they signal a fixed record.`],
[`What is a decorator?`,`A function that takes a function and returns a wrapped version, adding behaviour like logging, retries or auth without changing the original. Applied with @name.`],
[`What is the GIL?`,`CPython's Global Interpreter Lock lets only one thread execute bytecode at a time. Threads still help for I/O-bound work; use multiprocessing for CPU-bound work.`],
[`Generator vs iterator?`,`An iterator implements __iter__ and __next__. A generator is an easy way to write one with yield: it's lazy and keeps its state between calls.`],
[`Shallow vs deep copy?`,`A shallow copy duplicates the outer container but shares the inner objects. copy.deepcopy duplicates everything recursively.`],
[`How does a dict work?`,`It's a hash table: the key's hash picks a slot, giving O(1) average lookup. Keys must be hashable. Insertion order is kept since Python 3.7.`],
[`What is a context manager?`,`An object with __enter__ and __exit__, used in a with block to guarantee set-up and clean-up. contextlib.contextmanager builds one from a generator.`],
[`== vs is?`,`== compares values; is compares identity (the same object). Use is only for None, True and False.`],
[`How do you manage dependencies?`,`A virtual environment per project and pinned versions (requirements with hashes, or pyproject plus a lock file via pip-tools, Poetry or uv), so CI builds are reproducible.`],
[`What are *args and **kwargs?`,`Extra positional arguments as a tuple and extra keyword arguments as a dict — used for flexible APIs and for passing arguments through wrappers.`]],
ops:[
[`What happens when you type a URL and hit enter?`,`DNS resolves the name, then a TCP handshake, a TLS handshake, and the HTTP request goes through a load balancer to the app. The response returns and the browser renders it. Mention caches and CDNs.`],
[`Container vs VM?`,`A VM virtualises hardware and runs a full OS. Containers share the host kernel and isolate processes with namespaces and cgroups, so they're lighter and start in seconds.`],
[`Walk me through a CI/CD pipeline you built.`,`Trigger → build → test → scan → image/artifact → deploy to staging → checks → prod via canary or approval → monitoring with rollback. End with a number, like time saved per release.`],
[`How does a Kubernetes Service find its pods?`,`Through a label selector. It gets a stable virtual IP and DNS name and load-balances across ready pods with matching labels.`],
[`How do you handle a production incident?`,`Acknowledge, assess impact, mitigate first (rollback, scale, failover), communicate status, then find the root cause and run a blameless postmortem with owned actions.`],
[`SLI vs SLO vs SLA?`,`The SLI is the measurement, the SLO is the internal target, and the SLA is the contract with customers, with penalties. The SLA is usually looser than the SLO.`],
[`What is IaC and why use it?`,`Defining infrastructure in code (Terraform, CloudFormation, Ansible). Environments become versioned, reviewable and repeatable, with less drift.`],
[`How do you manage secrets?`,`Never in code. Use a secret manager (Vault, a cloud secrets service, encrypted Kubernetes Secrets), least privilege, rotation and audit logs.`],
[`Monitoring vs observability?`,`Monitoring watches known failure signals. Observability lets you ask new questions about a system using metrics, logs and traces together.`],
[`How do you shrink a Docker image?`,`A slim or distroless base, multi-stage builds, a .dockerignore, fewer layers, and cleaning package caches in the same RUN step.`]],
auto:[
[`Walk me through your automation framework.`,`Stack, then layers (config, utilities, API/page objects, tests, test data), reporting, how CI triggers it, parallel runs, and impact with a number.`],
[`How do you handle flaky tests?`,`Reproduce (repeat runs, random order), find the cause (timing, shared state, environment), fix with explicit waits and isolation, quarantine meanwhile, and track the flake rate.`],
[`What do you automate first?`,`Stable, repetitive, high-value cases: smoke and regression suites, API before UI, critical user journeys. Skip one-offs and features still changing fast.`],
[`Fixtures vs setUp/tearDown?`,`Fixtures are injected by name, reusable, scoped from function to session and composable; yield handles teardown. Cleaner than xUnit-style setUp.`],
[`Mock vs stub vs fake?`,`A stub returns canned data. A mock also records calls so you can assert on them. A fake is a working lightweight version, like an in-memory database.`],
[`How do you test a REST API?`,`Status codes, response schema and values, error cases, auth, boundaries and idempotency, with fixtures that create and clean up test data.`],
[`How do you run tests in parallel safely?`,`pytest-xdist with -n auto, tests that share no state, unique test data per worker, and isolated environments or containers.`],
[`Where do automated tests fit in CI?`,`Unit tests on every commit, API and integration tests on merge, full regression nightly or before release. Results are published and gate the pipeline.`]],
scn:[
[`Your service pages on-call every night, and every morning the alert is closed with no action. What do you change?`,`Those alerts aren't actionable. Alert on user-facing symptoms tied to SLOs (error rate, latency burn rate), not causes like CPU. Delete or downgrade noisy alerts, add a runbook to every page, and review alert quality after each rotation.`],
[`How would you migrate a pile of Bash scripts to Infrastructure as Code?`,`Audit first: what each script creates and who runs it. Import existing resources into Terraform state, start with low-risk pieces, build reusable modules, run plan in CI on every PR, and retire scripts one by one once plan shows no drift.`],
[`A change passed every test but broke production. What now?`,`Mitigate first: roll back or turn the feature flag off. Then a blameless review of why tests couldn't catch it (data shape, config, scale, a dependency). Close the gap with a canary, realistic staging data, contract tests or a smoke check.`],
[`Latency on one API doubled right after a deploy. Walk me through it.`,`Confirm scope in metrics (endpoints, regions, p50 vs p99). Compare with the deploy diff. Use traces to find the slow span — DB, a downstream call. Check saturation. If users are hurting, roll back first and investigate after.`],
[`A Kubernetes node goes NotReady. What do you check?`,`kubectl describe node for memory, disk or PID pressure; kubelet and container-runtime logs; network to the control plane; the VM in the cloud console. Cordon and drain if needed — controller-managed pods reschedule elsewhere.`],
[`Design CI/CD for 20 developers shipping a microservice.`,`PR checks (lint, unit tests, SAST). Build one image tagged with the commit SHA, push to a registry, auto-deploy to staging, run integration and smoke tests, then promote the same image to prod via GitOps with a canary and automatic rollback on SLO breach.`],
[`A test suite takes 3 hours. Get it to 30 minutes.`,`Measure where time goes. Parallelise (pytest-xdist, sharding across runners), push UI checks down to API level, remove sleeps, share expensive set-up via session fixtures, run only impacted tests on PRs and the full suite nightly.`],
[`A disk fills up on a production server at 2 a.m. What do you do?`,`Free space safely first (compress or rotate logs, clear temp files, check deleted-but-open files with lsof). Then fix the cause (log level, runaway job), set rotation and retention, and alert at 80% so nobody gets woken at 100%.`]],
beh:[
[`Tell me about yourself.`,`90 seconds: current role and scope, two wins with numbers, the skills that match this role, and why you're looking now. Write and rehearse yours in Roles → My stories.`],
[`Tell me about a time you made a mistake.`,`Pick a real, moderate mistake. Own it plainly, show how you limited the damage, what you changed so it can't recur, and what you learned. No fake weaknesses.`],
[`Tell me about a disagreement with a teammate.`,`Describe it neutrally, how you listened and used data, how it was decided, and that the working relationship stayed good afterwards.`],
[`Why are you leaving your current company?`,`Stay positive and forward-looking: bigger scope, ownership of reliability or platform work, growth. Never criticise your current employer.`],
[`Describe a process you improved.`,`Before (the pain, with a number) → what you changed → how you got buy-in → after (the number). Automation stories fit perfectly.`],
[`Where do you see yourself in three years?`,`Tie it to this role: deeper ownership, leading initiatives, mentoring others. Ambition that fits their team.`]
/*ADD-BEH*/],
con:[
[`Explain how a HashMap / Python dict works.`,`Keys are hashed to pick a bucket in an array, so lookup is O(1) on average. Collisions (two keys, one slot) are resolved by probing or chaining. Keys must be immutable so their hash never changes. Worst case O(n) if everything collides.`],
[`Process vs thread vs coroutine?`,`A process has its own memory; threads share a process's memory and need locks; coroutines take turns on one thread, switching at await. Processes for CPU-bound work, threads or asyncio for I/O-bound.`],
[`How does a container differ from a VM?`,`A container is an isolated Linux process: namespaces control what it sees, cgroups limit what it uses, and it shares the host kernel. A VM runs its own full OS on virtual hardware. Containers start in seconds with little overhead.`],
[`Explain Kubernetes architecture.`,`Control plane: API server (front door), etcd (state), scheduler (places pods), controller-manager (reconcile loops). Each node runs kubelet (starts pods), kube-proxy (Service routing) and a container runtime. You declare desired state; controllers keep reality matching it.`],
[`What happens in a TLS handshake?`,`Client and server agree on a cipher, the server sends its certificate, the client verifies it against trusted CAs, and both derive shared session keys. After that, traffic is encrypted and tamper-evident.`],
[`L4 vs L7 load balancing?`,`L4 forwards by IP and port without reading the payload — fast and protocol-agnostic. L7 understands HTTP, so it can route by path or host, terminate TLS, and add retries or header rules.`],
[`What is idempotency and why does it matter?`,`Repeating an operation has the same effect as doing it once. It makes retries safe — critical in distributed systems where you can't tell if a timed-out request actually succeeded.`],
[`Explain the CAP theorem.`,`When a network partition happens, a distributed system must choose between consistency (every read sees the latest write) and availability (every request gets an answer). Banks often pick C; social feeds often pick A.`],
[`Declarative vs imperative infrastructure?`,`Imperative scripts list the steps ("create VM, then disk"). Declarative tools (Terraform, Kubernetes YAML) describe the end state and work out the steps, so runs are repeatable and drift can be detected.`],
[`Equivalence partitioning vs boundary value analysis?`,`Partitioning groups inputs that behave the same and tests one from each group. Boundary analysis tests at and just around each edge, where off-by-one bugs live. Used together they give high coverage with few tests.`],
[`What is technical debt in test automation?`,`Shortcuts that make the suite slower or flakier over time — hard-coded waits, duplicated locators, shared test data. Pay it down with page objects, fixtures, explicit waits and regular pruning.`]
/*ADD-CON*/],
rep:[
[`Describe your understanding of HashMap.`,`Hash the key to choose a bucket, store the pair there; lookup is O(1) on average. Mention collisions, why keys must be immutable, load factor and resizing — and that Python's dict is a hash map.`,[`HSBC DevOps Engineer · Glassdoor`,`https://www.glassdoor.com/Interview/HSBC-Interview-E3482-RVW94598130.htm`]],
[`Security questions between services — how do services trust each other?`,`Mutual TLS (both sides present certificates), short-lived tokens (OAuth2/JWT) checked at a gateway or sidecar, least-privilege service identities, and network policies so only allowed services can connect. Secrets come from a vault, never from code.`,[`HSBC · Glassdoor`,`https://www.glassdoor.co.uk/Interview/HSBC-Interview-E3482-RVW94235345.htm`]],
[`Network protocols and cloud basics.`,`Be ready for: TCP vs UDP, the DNS lookup chain, TLS, HTTP status codes, subnets and CIDR, NAT, load balancers, and IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS — with one real example of each from your work.`,[`Microsoft SRE/DevOps, Hyderabad · Glassdoor`,`https://www.glassdoor.com.hk/Interview/Microsoft-Interview-E1651-RVW93863660.htm`]],
[`Tell me about a time you made a mistake. How did you resolve it?`,`STAR: a real, moderate mistake → how you spotted and contained it → the process fix so it can't recur → what you learned. Own it in the first sentence.`,[`Microsoft SRE · Glassdoor`,`https://www.glassdoor.co.in/Interview/Microsoft-Interview-E1651-RVW89692778.htm`]],
[`How does Redis ensure persistence?`,`Two options: RDB snapshots (point-in-time dumps at intervals — compact, but you can lose recent writes) and AOF (append-only log of every write, fsynced per second or per write — safer, larger). Many setups use both, plus replication.`,[`Microsoft SWE II (SRE), Hyderabad · Glassdoor`,`https://www.glassdoor.com.au/Interview/Microsoft-Interview-E1651-RVW34598230.htm`]],
[`How would you autoscale pods without using CPU or memory?`,`HPA with custom metrics (requests per second via Prometheus Adapter) or external metrics (queue length), or KEDA, which scales on event sources like Kafka lag or SQS depth — even down to zero.`,[`Deloitte DevOps, Hyderabad · Taro`,`https://www.jointaro.com/interviews/companies/deloitte/experiences/devops-engineer-hyderabad-telangana-november-1-2025-no-offer-positive-a2891765`]],
[`What is kube-proxy? Deployment vs StatefulSet?`,`kube-proxy runs on every node and programs iptables/IPVS so Service IPs reach healthy pods. Deployments manage interchangeable pods; StatefulSets give each pod a stable name and its own volume — for databases and brokers.`,[`DevOps/SRE interview post · LinkedIn`,`https://mx.linkedin.com/in/vanessa-mromero`]],
[`How does Terraform state locking work, and what is drift?`,`Locking stops two applies writing state at once (e.g. a lock record alongside an S3 backend). Drift means real resources changed outside Terraform; plan shows it, and you reconcile by updating code or re-applying.`,[`DevOps/SRE interview post · LinkedIn`,`https://mx.linkedin.com/in/vanessa-mromero`]],
[`When do you use terraform import?`,`To bring an existing resource (created by hand or another tool) under Terraform management without recreating it. Newer Terraform also supports import blocks in code, reviewed through plan.`,[`DevOps/SRE interview post · LinkedIn`,`https://mx.linkedin.com/in/vanessa-mromero`]],
[`count vs for_each — and moving state without recreating resources?`,`count indexes by number, so removing one item shifts and recreates the rest; for_each keys by name and stays stable. To rename or move resources, use terraform state mv or a moved block.`,[`DevOps interview post · LinkedIn`,`https://eg.linkedin.com/in/shymaa-saeed1`]],
[`You launched an app on AWS; NAT is set up but it still can't reach the internet. What next?`,`Check the route table points 0.0.0.0/0 at the NAT, then security groups and network ACLs (outbound and return ports), DNS resolution, and the NAT's own subnet route to an internet gateway.`,[`Wipro DevOps interview post · LinkedIn`,`https://bd.linkedin.com/in/arifur-rahman-duet`]],
[`What are taints and tolerations?`,`A taint on a node repels pods; only pods with a matching toleration can schedule there. Used to reserve nodes (GPU, system workloads). Node affinity is the opposite: it attracts pods to nodes.`,[`Wipro DevOps interview post · LinkedIn`,`https://bd.linkedin.com/in/arifur-rahman-duet`]],
[`If you receive 10,000 requests, how does traffic flow through your architecture?`,`DNS → CDN/WAF → load balancer → ingress → stateless app pods (autoscaled) → cache → database/replicas, with queues for slow work. Mention where you'd watch metrics and what saturates first.`,[`Forage.ai DevOps interview post · LinkedIn`,`https://ar.linkedin.com/in/matias-ezequiel-silva`]],
[`Explain what Kubernetes is and how it manages containers.`,`A container orchestrator: you declare desired state; the scheduler places pods on nodes, controllers restart or replace failed ones, Services give stable networking, and it scales and rolls out updates.`,[`Cognizant DevOps, Hyderabad (Feb 2026) · Glassdoor`,`https://static.glassdoor.at/Interview/Cognizant-Devops-Engineer-Intern-Interview-Questions-EI_IE8014.0,9_KO10,32.htm`]],
[`Write Python to move all zeroes to the right of a list.`,`Collect non-zeroes in order, then append the right number of zeroes — or a two-pointer in-place swap for O(1) extra space. It's in the Code tab as "Move zeroes".`,[`SDET interview · Glassdoor`,`https://static.glassdoor.com.mx/Interview/sdet-interview-questions-SRCH_KO0,4_IP402.htm`]],
[`How would you handle a project where you're the only QA?`,`Risk-based priorities, automate smoke and API checks first, push unit tests to developers, add a CI quality gate, and make quality visible with simple dashboards.`,[`SDET interview · Glassdoor`,`https://static.glassdoor.com.mx/Interview/sdet-interview-questions-SRCH_KO0,4_IP402.htm`]],
[`What specific challenges have you faced in an automation project?`,`Pick one real challenge (flaky environment, unstable locators, slow suite, test data), what you did, and the measurable result.`,[`Amgen Automation Engineer · Glassdoor`,`https://www.glassdoor.ca/Interview/Amgen-Interview-E1130-RVW88606577.htm`]],
[`Why Amgen? Why are you a good fit?`,`Connect their mission and the role's stack (Python, APIs, test automation, validation) to your concrete experience, then one thing you'd bring in the first 90 days.`,[`Amgen · Glassdoor`,`https://clear.glassdoor.nl/Interview/Amgen-Process-Automation-Engineer-Interview-Questions-EI_IE1130.0,5_KO6,33.htm`]],
[`Describe a time you disagreed with management.`,`Show respect and data: the disagreement, how you made your case, how the decision was made, and how you committed to it — and what happened.`,[`Amgen Associate Engineer · Glassdoor`,`https://www.glassdoor.com/Interview/Amgen-Interview-E1130-RVW95369798.htm`]],
[`Difference between deep and shallow copy in Python?`,`Shallow copy duplicates the outer container but shares nested objects; deepcopy duplicates everything recursively. Show it with a nested list.`,[`DevOps interview questions · AmbitionBox`,`https://ambitionbox.com/profiles/devops/interview-questions?page=2`]]
/*ADD-REP*/]};
