const QA2={
sto:[
[`Block vs file vs object storage — when would you use each?`,`Block for databases and VMs needing low-latency raw volumes (SAN: FC, iSCSI, NVMe-oF). File for shared folders and apps that need a POSIX or SMB share (NAS: NFS, SMB). Object for massive, cheap, metadata-rich data like backups, logs and media (S3 API).`],
[`Explain RPO and RTO with an example.`,`RPO is how much data you can lose: hourly backups mean up to an hour lost. RTO is how fast you must be back: a 30-minute RTO needs a warm standby and a tested runbook. A business impact analysis sets both per system.`],
[`Snapshot vs backup?`,`A snapshot is a quick point-in-time view on the same system — great for rollback but shares the array's fate. A backup is an independent copy on other media or another site, ideally immutable. You want both.`],
[`What is the 3-2-1-1-0 rule?`,`Three copies of data, on two media types, one off-site, one immutable or offline, and zero errors when you test restores.`],
[`RAID 5 vs RAID 6 vs RAID 10?`,`RAID 5: single parity, survives one disk, n−1 capacity. RAID 6: double parity, survives two, n−2. RAID 10: mirrored stripes, fast writes, survives one per mirror, half the capacity. Big HDDs favour RAID 6 because rebuilds take long.`],
[`How does deduplication work and when does it fail?`,`Data is chunked and hashed; only new chunks are stored, repeats become pointers. It fails on already compressed or encrypted data, because identical content no longer looks identical.`],
[`Synchronous vs asynchronous replication?`,`Synchronous waits for the remote copy before acknowledging — RPO near zero but limited by distance/latency. Asynchronous acknowledges locally and ships later — any distance, RPO equals the replication lag.`],
[`How would you make backups ransomware-resilient?`,`Immutable (WORM / object lock) copies, an isolated or air-gapped copy, separate MFA-protected backup admin accounts, encryption, alerts on mass deletion or retention changes, and regular restore tests.`]],
sec:[
[`Explain the CIA triad with one attack for each.`,`Confidentiality — a data leak; integrity — tampering with records; availability — DDoS or ransomware. Controls: encryption and access control; hashing and signatures; redundancy and backups.`],
[`Symmetric vs asymmetric encryption — how does TLS use both?`,`Asymmetric (RSA/ECC) authenticates the server's certificate and agrees on keys; symmetric (AES) then encrypts the bulk traffic because it's much faster.`],
[`How should passwords be stored?`,`Never reversibly. Use a slow, salted hash designed for passwords — bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2 or PBKDF2 — with a unique salt per user, plus MFA and breached-password checks.`],
[`What is zero trust?`,`No implicit trust from network location. Every request is verified using identity, device posture and policy, with least privilege and continuous monitoring — assume breach.`],
[`Walk me through incident response.`,`Preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery and lessons learned (Security+). NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 3 (2025) frames this within CSF 2.0's Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover functions.`],
[`What's in the OWASP Top 10:2025?`,`A01 Broken Access Control, A02 Security Misconfiguration, A03 Software Supply Chain Failures, A04 Cryptographic Failures, A05 Injection … A09 Security Logging & Alerting Failures, A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions.`],
[`How do you prioritise vulnerabilities?`,`Severity (CVSS) plus real-world exploitation, exposure (internet-facing?), asset criticality and available mitigations. An exploited high on the internet beats an unexploited critical on an isolated box.`],
[`Risk responses?`,`Mitigate (add controls), transfer (insurance or contract), avoid (stop the activity), accept (documented sign-off). Quantify with ALE = SLE × ARO.`]]};
Object.entries(QA2).forEach(([tr,arr])=>arr.forEach(([q,a],i)=>CARDS.push({k:tr+i,tr,q,a})));
